.TH MQ_OPEN 3 "July 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
mq_open, mq_close, mq_unlink \- open, close, and remove a POSIX message queue
.SH LIBRARY
Realtime library (librt, \-lrt)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <mqueue.h>
.PP
.BI "mqd_t mq_open(const char *" name ", int " oflag );
.br
.BI "mqd_t mq_open(const char *" name ", int " oflag ", mode_t " mode ", struct mq_attr *" attr );
.br
.BI "int mq_close(mqd_t " mqdes );
.br
.BI "int mq_unlink(const char *" name );
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR mq_open ()
creates a new POSIX message queue or opens an existing one.  The queue is
identified by
.IR name ,
a string that must begin with a slash
.RB ( / )
and contain no other slash (for example
.BR /myqueue ).
The
.I oflag
argument is a bitwise-or of an access mode
.RB ( O_RDONLY ,
.BR O_WRONLY ,
or
.BR O_RDWR )
and any of the flags
.BR O_CREAT ,
.BR O_EXCL ,
and
.BR O_NONBLOCK .
.PP
When
.B O_CREAT
is set, two further arguments are supplied: a
.I mode
(the permission bits, modified by the process umask) and a pointer to a
.B struct mq_attr
that specifies
.I mq_maxmsg
(maximum number of messages) and
.I mq_msgsize
(maximum message size in bytes).  A NULL
.I attr
selects the implementation defaults.  The other
.B mq_attr
fields are ignored on creation.
.PP
.BR mq_open ()
returns a message-queue descriptor
.RB ( mqd_t )
that refers to the open queue.  The descriptor is used by all the other
.B mq_*
functions.  Substrate implements the queue in the kernel; the descriptor is
an opaque small integer drawn from a per-open table.
.PP
.BR mq_close ()
closes the descriptor
.IR mqdes ,
releasing the reference the calling process holds on the queue.  Any
notification request the process registered with
.BR mq_notify (3)
on this descriptor is removed.
.PP
.BR mq_unlink ()
removes the queue
.IR name .
The name is removed immediately, but the queue itself is destroyed only once
every descriptor referring to it has been closed.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR mq_open ()
returns a message-queue descriptor (a non-negative
.BR mqd_t ).
.BR mq_close ()
and
.BR mq_unlink ()
return 0.  On error all three return
.RB ( mqd_t )
.B \-1
and set
.IR errno .
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
The queue exists but the caller lacks the requested permission, or
.I name
contains more than one slash.
.TP
.B EEXIST
Both
.B O_CREAT
and
.B O_EXCL
were set but the queue already exists.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I name
is malformed, or
.B O_CREAT
was set with an invalid
.I attr
.RB ( mq_maxmsg
or
.I mq_msgsize
out of range).
.TP
.B EMFILE
The system-wide table of open message-queue descriptors is full.
.TP
.B ENOENT
.B O_CREAT
was not set and the named queue does not exist, or
.BR mq_unlink ()
was called on a name that does not exist.
.TP
.B ENOSPC
No space is left in the system-wide message-queue table.
.TP
.B EBADF
.RB ( mq_close )
.I mqdes
is not a valid open message-queue descriptor.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
struct mq_attr attr = { 0, 8, 64, 0 };   /* 8 msgs, 64 bytes each */
mqd_t q = mq_open("/myqueue", O_CREAT | O_RDWR, 0644, &attr);
if (q == (mqd_t)\-1) { perror("mq_open"); return 1; }
/* ... mq_send() / mq_receive() ... */
mq_close(q);
mq_unlink("/myqueue");
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mq_send (3),
.BR mq_receive (3),
.BR mq_notify (3),
.BR mq_getattr (3),
.BR mq_open (2)
